<h2 id="the-problem">Why Gerund vs Infinitive Feels Harder Than It Is</h2>
<p>Here's what I've noticed after two decades of teaching this exact topic: students can recite “enjoy takes gerund” and “want takes infinitive”, yet they still choose the wrong form when the sentence looks slightly different or when a verb can take both. The real problem is not memory — it is the lack of a clear decision order.</p>
<ul>
<li>You know many verbs but freeze when you meet a new one in the exam.</li>
<li>You mix up the verbs that change meaning with gerund and infinitive.</li>
<li>You rely on what “sounds right” in spoken Indian English, which is often wrong in written exams.</li>
<li>You have never grouped the verbs into the three practical categories examiners use.</li>
<li>I know exactly how that feels — I watched this confusion cost students marks for 22 years before I started teaching the forms as three clear groups instead of one long list.</li>
</ul>
<p>But here's what most people get wrong: they try to memorise every verb separately instead of learning the three patterns that cover almost every question.</p>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">3 Mistakes That Cost the Most Marks</h2>

<p><strong>1. Using infinitive after gerund-only verbs.</strong><br>Verbs such as enjoy, finish, avoid, mind, keep, practise and suggest are followed only by the gerund. Adding “to” is one of the most common errors in Bank PO papers.</p>

<p><strong>2. Using gerund after infinitive-only verbs.</strong><br>Verbs such as want, decide, hope, plan, refuse, learn and promise take only the infinitive. Students often put -ing out of habit.</p>

<p><strong>3. Ignoring the meaning change with dual verbs.</strong><br>Stop, remember, forget, try, regret and go on change meaning depending on the form that follows. Examiners test this distinction every year.</p>

