<h2 id="the-solution">The Complete Gerund vs Infinitive System, Step by Step</h2>
<p>Treat the choice as three clear groups instead of one long list. Once the groups are in your head, most exam questions become straightforward.</p>

<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="exam-table">
<thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Form Required</th><th>Common Verbs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Gerund only</td><td>-ing</td><td>enjoy, finish, avoid, mind, keep, practise, suggest, consider, delay, miss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Infinitive only</td><td>to + verb</td><td>want, decide, hope, plan, refuse, learn, promise, agree, afford, manage</td></tr>
<tr><td>Both (meaning changes)</td><td>gerund or infinitive</td><td>stop, remember, forget, try, regret, go on</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p><strong>Step 1: Identify whether the verb belongs to the gerund-only group.</strong> If it does, never use the infinitive.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>She avoids to speak in public.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>She avoids <strong>speaking</strong> in public.</em> (avoid takes only gerund)</div>
</div>

<p><strong>Step 2: Check for the infinitive-only group.</strong> Want, decide, hope, plan and similar verbs are followed only by to + verb.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>They hope getting good marks.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>They hope <strong>to get</strong> good marks.</em> (hope takes only infinitive)</div>
</div>

<p><strong>Step 3: Master the dual verbs that change meaning.</strong> These are the highest-value questions in the exam.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>I remember to lock the door (when you mean a past memory).</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>I remember <strong>locking</strong> the door.</em> (memory of a past action)</div>
</div>

<p><strong>Step 4: Learn the fixed phrases that always take gerund.</strong> look forward to, be used to, object to, prefer + -ing, feel like + -ing.</p>

<p><strong>Step 5: Use infinitive of purpose when the sentence shows why someone does something.</strong></p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>He went to the market for buying vegetables.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>He went to the market <strong>to buy</strong> vegetables.</em> (purpose)</div>
</div>

<p><strong>Step 6: Practise the high-frequency pairs that appear in almost every paper.</strong></p>

