<h2 id="case-study">A Quick Turnaround Story</h2>
<p>One of my Bank PO students used to lose marks on almost every gerund-infinitive question. She knew many verbs but had no system. I asked her to follow only the three-group method for ten days and to write one example for each dual verb every morning. In the next mock her accuracy on this topic went from 50 % to nearly 100 %. Nothing else changed — only the way she looked at the sentence. You can do the same.</p>

<h2 id="expert-tips">Expert Tips From the Classroom</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Make two short lists on one page — gerund-only and infinitive-only.</strong> Revise them for two minutes before every mock.</li>
<li><strong>Treat the six dual verbs as special cases.</strong> Write both meanings for stop, remember, forget, try, regret and go on.</li>
<li><strong>Never decide by what sounds natural in speech.</strong> Spoken Indian English often mixes the forms; exam English does not.</li>
<li><strong>When you see “look forward to”, “be used to” or “object to”, the next word must be a gerund.</strong> These phrases appear every year.</li>
<li><strong>Practise error-spotting questions that mix the three groups.</strong> That is exactly how the exams test this topic.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="conclusion">The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Gerund or infinitive stops being a guessing game the moment you stop memorising one long list and start using three clear groups. Learn the gerund-only verbs, the infinitive-only verbs, and the six dual verbs that change meaning. That system covers almost every question you will meet in SSC CGL, Bank PO and Railway exams.</p>

